<h3>Estimated saving from changing the pension increase</h3>
<p>The nearest published estimate is a saving of {{ "saving-smoothed-rf-2030" | figShown }} in 2029-30 from a smoothed earnings link starting in 2027-28, after tax and benefit effects.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("saving-smoothed-rf-2030" | figure) }}

<p>Over the long term the saving grows. <a href="/reform-options/#cost-and-saving">Reform options: costs and savings</a> has the estimates and their bases.</p>

{% call m.note("What the scale shows, and what it does not", 3) %}
<p>In the first years after a change, the published estimate of the pension saving is a small part of the published estimate for universal care. The two estimates are for different years and in different prices, so the comparison is rough. It does not show that the trade-off is impossible, because a plan could be smaller, could start later, or could use other money as well. It does show that a plan would need to say where the rest comes from, and when.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h2 id="questions">Questions a proposal must answer</h2>

<p>No proposal has been published, so these questions cannot yet be answered. They are the test to apply when one is.</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>What rule would replace the triple lock, from when, and for whom?</strong> Including whether pensions already in payment are affected, and what happens in a year of high inflation.</li>
  <li><strong>What care would be covered?</strong> Personal care only, or accommodation too. At home, in a care home, or both.</li>
  <li><strong>Who would be eligible?</strong> Which level of need, which ages, which nations.</li>
  <li><strong>Who loses income, who receives a service, and when?</strong> Any loss depends on the rule chosen and on how prices and earnings move. It would be spread across all pensioners. The service goes to some people, at the time they need it.</li>
  <li><strong>What are the costs and savings, and against what baseline?</strong> Before or after tax and benefit effects. In cash or in constant prices. For one year or added up over several.</li>
  <li><strong>Would the saving actually be used for care?</strong> Spending is decided year by year. A saving in one budget is not tied to another unless the law or the plan ties it.</li>
  <li><strong>What pays for the early years?</strong> Care costs begin when the service begins. Pension savings build up slowly.</li>
  <li><strong>Can the service be delivered?</strong> Staff, care homes and councils would need to be in place.</li>
  <li><strong>What happens in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland?</strong></li>
  <li><strong>What happens to people on the lowest incomes?</strong> Including whether Pension Credit would rise to make up the difference, given that many who are entitled do not claim it.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="not-assume">What should not be assumed</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>That what the triple lock has cost is money now available.</strong> Past increases are in today's rates. Only future increases can change. <a href="/reform-options/#cost-and-saving">Past cost is not future saving</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>That each person who gives up pension income receives care of the same value.</strong> A pension change would reach every pensioner. A care service would reach the people who need care. Who gains and who loses depends on a design that has not been published.</li>
  <li><strong>That changing the rule means a cut in pounds.</strong> Under the alternatives discussed, the pension would still rise each year. <a href="/reform-options/#compare">Compare the rules</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>That a think tank's estimate is the cost of a government plan.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>That a report of what a politician will say is what they said.</strong></li>
</ul>

<p>{{ ("hf-affordable" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("hf-affordable") }} That is a view about priorities. Others weigh the same figures differently.</p>
